{"id":"four-colors","title":"Four Colors","url":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Four_Colors","rev":238632,"fetched":"2026-10-16","lead":[{"t":"p","c":[{"t":"b","c":["Four Colors"]}," was a frequently used color scheme found in consumer products and consumer technology during the Mid-2000s to the Mid-2010s, first primarily seen around 2004 as a part of Apple's iPod \"Silhouette\" advertising campaign. It is a subgenre of ",{"t":"a","c":["Superflat Pop"],"id":"superflat-pop"}," and ",{"t":"a","c":["Frutiger Aero"],"id":"frutiger-aero"}," and prominently consists of the colors electric lime, sky blue, hot pink and neon orange. This color palette and the visuals of the aesthetic overlaps with ",{"t":"a","c":["Bright Tertiaries"],"id":"bright-tertiaries"},", which uses tertiary colors like teal, lime green, and orange or fuchsia, as well as flat humanist designs. ",{"t":"b","c":["Four Colors"]}," is popular in Japan with stores and brands such as Daiso, Sony and Fujifilm."]}],"sections":[{"title":"History","id":"history","blocks":[{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Origins (Late-1990s/Early-2000s)"]},{"t":"p","c":[{"t":"b","c":["Four Colors"]},"' roots dates back to the late-1990s, forming as parts of ",{"t":"a","c":["Superflat Pop"],"id":"superflat-pop"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["Y2K Futurism"],"id":"y2k-futurism"},", and primitive ",{"t":"a","c":["Frutiger Aero"],"id":"frutiger-aero"},". During this primitive period, ",{"t":"b","c":["Four Colors"]}," was primarily used for different-color variants of video game consoles, primarily the ",{"t":"a","c":["Nintendo 64"],"href":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_64"},"."]},{"t":"img","img":{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/d/dd/N64colors.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20230823154229","w":180,"h":237,"cap":[{"t":"a","c":["Nintendo 64"],"href":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_64"}," (1996) color variants - featuring the \"Funtastic Series\" containing 6 consoles, as well as featuring other color variants."]},"side":"center"},{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Prime (2000s)"]},{"t":"p","c":[{"t":"b","c":["Four Colors"]},"' prime began in October 2003 when Apple began the \"Silhouette\" iPod advertising campaign. Apple reportedly spent $49.6 million on the ‘‘Silhouette’’ campaign between January and August of 2004, being majorly influential on ",{"t":"b","c":["Four Colors"]},"' widespread success and adoption in ",{"t":"a","c":["the West"],"href":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_world"},". ",{"t":"b","c":["Four Colors"]}," continued to be heavily popular during the \"Frutiger\" time period, generally seeing increased use in consumer technology (eg. ",{"t":"a","c":["iPod"],"href":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IPod"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["Wii"],"href":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wii"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["PlayStation 2"],"href":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PlayStation_2"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["3"],"href":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PlayStation_3"},", etc.) as well as miscellaneous advertising."]},{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Decline (Mid 2010s)"]},{"t":"p","c":[{"t":"b","c":["Four Colors"]}," continued to be popular until the mid-2010s, when the Frutiger Family of aesthetics were in decline. Companies began focusing more on ",{"t":"a","c":["Flat Design"],"id":"flat-design"}," and being uniform, omitting the personalization and eye-catching color combinations. By 2017, ",{"t":"a","c":["Flat Design"],"id":"flat-design"}," and other aesthetics such as ",{"t":"a","c":["Corporate Memphis"],"id":"corporate-memphis"}," had completely phased out ",{"t":"b","c":["Four Colors"]},", giving way to a new era."]},{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Japan (Post-2014/15)"]},{"t":"p","c":[{"t":"b","c":["Four Colors"]}," still sees varied degrees of success in Japan and is still relatively popular despite its decline in ",{"t":"a","c":["the West"],"href":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_world"},"."]}]},{"title":"Visuals","id":"visuals","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":["The visuals of ",{"t":"b","c":["Four Colors"]}," consist of flat visuals that predominantly use four colors: Electric lime, sky blue, hot pink, neon orange; ",{"t":"b","c":["Four Colors"]},"' color palette is also noted as being a reference to the colors of different seasons in Japan, specifically: Pink = spring, green = summer, orange = autumn, and blue = winter. ",{"t":"b","c":["Four Colors"]}," can also host a wider range of colors too, forming a rainbow. However, the main colors are usually the ones mentioned above. These visuals are often utilized in conjunction with consumer products or corporate designs. ",{"t":"b","c":["Four Colors"]}," is also sometimes featured in video game box-art."]}]},{"title":"Media","id":"media","blocks":[{"t":"h","l":4,"c":["Music Videos"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":[{"t":"a","c":["Electrosaturator - tilt-six feat. Hatsune Miku"],"href":"https://youtu.be/hN2PJ8mBF6w?si=h8uIIaYcKcxUw1-B"}]},{"c":[{"t":"a","c":["Lollipop - 2NE1 Ft. Bigbang"],"href":"https://youtu.be/zIRW_elc-rY?si=gILFdlRm5F3NMh3p"}]}]},{"t":"h","l":4,"c":["Television"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Eurovision Song Contest 2007"]}]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["The Fresh Beat Band"]}," (2009-2013)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["The Backyardigans"]}," (2004-2013)"]}]},{"t":"h","l":4,"c":["Anime"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Life Lessons with Uramichi Oniisan"]}]}]},{"t":"h","l":4,"c":["Electronics"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["Nintendo 64 (1996)"]},{"c":["iPod Nano/Shuffle (2005)"]},{"c":["Motorola Pebl (2005)"]},{"c":["Nintendo Wii (2006)"]},{"c":["iPhone 5C (2013)"]}]},{"t":"h","l":4,"c":["Video Games"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Katamari"]}," series (2004–2023)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Just Dance"]}," series (2009–2024)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Just Shapes & Beats"]}," (2018)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Vitamin Connection"]}," (2020)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Wii Party"]}," (2010)"]}]}]},{"title":"Gallery","id":"gallery","blocks":[{"t":"gallery","items":[{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/im